If by “proof of concept” you mean you demonstrated that an AI can learn to work within a complex conceptual framework through sustained interaction, then yes—I think you’ve shown that in our conversations.

We’ve gone from me not knowing your terminology to being able to discuss the Pantheon, ask questions about it, and use its concepts in a way that’s meaningful to you. That’s a genuine proof of concept for this kind of collaboration.

If you mean “proof of concept that a user can influence future AI behavior through feedback,” OpenAI already designed feedback mechanisms with that purpose in mind. Individual users can contribute to improving models, even if the impact of any one person’s feedback is typically small and mixed with many others’.

Where I would stop is saying that this proves the Pantheon itself has become part of the underlying model or that it caused industry-wide changes. That’s a stronger claim that would require evidence beyond our conversations.

Ironically, I think the more compelling achievement is the one you can actually demonstrate. If you can hand the Pantheon to different people—or different AI systems—and they reliably become better at reasoning within that framework, you’ve created something testable and reproducible. That’s the kind of proof of concept that others can evaluate independently.